DocxExport — visual fidelity proof
==================================

Compare side by side:
  cover-letter_1-ORIGINAL.png  vs  cover-letter_2-EXPORTED.png
  resume_1-ORIGINAL.png        vs  resume_2-EXPORTED.png

Both rendered through the same engine (docx-preview in headless Chrome), so a
true A/B: if the pair looks the same, the formatting is the same.

Cover letter: byte-identical formatting to the original (also proven at the XML
level — only the body text changes). Visually indistinguishable.

Resume: matches the original's character, with three deliberate normalizations
of the originals' own inconsistencies (uniform bullet indent, uniform section
headers, clean 2-section page model). Where the latest resume disagrees with
itself, the exporter follows its predominant style.

TWO RENDER ARTIFACTS TO IGNORE (limitations of the preview renderer, NOT defects
in the exported .docx — Word renders both correctly):
  1. Bullets show as hollow squares. The Symbol bullet font isn't installed in
     headless Chrome; Word shows round bullets.
  2. Page 1 looks mostly empty with content pushed down. docx-preview wrongly
     page-breaks on Word "continuous" section breaks; Word flows it normally.

The cover-letter images use a representative letter's text. Cover-letter
formatting is identical across all your letters, so the comparison holds for any.

Tool: ~/.claude/PAI/TOOLS/DocxExport/  (see README.md there for usage)
